{"name":"Beauty Patch","hex":"#834f44","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beauty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quality of being (especially visually) attractive, pleasing, fine or good-looking; comeliness.","Someone who is beautiful."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Thanks!","Cool!"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beauty"},{"term":"patch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of cloth, or other suitable material, sewed or otherwise fixed upon a garment to repair or strengthen it, especially upon an old garment to cover a hole.","A small piece of anything used to repair damage or a breach; as, a patch on a kettle, a roof, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mend by sewing on a piece or pieces of cloth, leather, or the like.","To mend with pieces; to repair by fastening pieces on."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/patch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beauty mark","blocks":[{"text":"A beauty mark or beauty spot is a euphemism for a type of dark facial mark so named because such birthmarks are sometimes considered an attractive feature. Medically, such \"beauty marks\" are generally melanocytic nevus, more specifically the compound variant. Moles of this type may also be located elsewhere on the body, and may also be considered beauty marks if located on the face, shoulder, neck or breast. Artificial beauty marks have been fashionable in some periods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/CindyCrawfordOct09.jpg/330px-CindyCrawfordOct09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty_mark","wikidata":"Q167078"},{"title":"Beauty","blocks":[{"text":"Beauty is commonly described as a feature of objects that makes them pleasurable to perceive. Such objects include landscapes, sunsets, humans and works of art. Beauty, art and taste are the main subjects of aesthetics, one of the fields of study within philosophy. As a positive aesthetic value, it is contrasted with ugliness as its negative counterpart."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg/330px-Rozeta_Pary%C5%BC_notre-dame_chalger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beauty","wikidata":"Q7242"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg/500px-Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg/500px-Anon_%28English_School%29_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Allegorical Painting of Two Ladies, English School, c. 1650, by an anonymous hand. The two women, who appear to be of equal standing, are wearing face patches, which were a fashion of the time. The painting is captioned \"I black with white bespott y white with blacke this evil proceeds from thy proud hart then take her: Devill.\"[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnon_(English_School)_-_double_portrait_-_circa_1650.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_Rosa_de_Rivera.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Mar%C3%ADa_